Let X be a set of real numbers (more generally a separable metrizable space). A countable cover U of X by Borel subsets of X with X ∉ U is called:
• a Borel ω-cover (U ∈ B_Ω) if every finite F ⊆ X is contained in some U ∈ U;
• a Borel γ-cover (U ∈ B_Γ) if U is infinite and every x ∈ X belongs to all but finitely many U ∈ U;
• a Borel τ-cover (U ∈ B_T) if U is a Borel ω-cover and for any two x, y ∈ X, either {U ∈ U : x ∈ U, y ∉ U} is finite or {U ∈ U : y ∈ U, x ∉ U} is finite;
• simply a (countable) Borel cover (U ∈ B) if X = ⋃ U.

For classes A, B of covers of X, define the selection principles:
• S1(A, B): for every sequence (U_n)_{n∈ℕ} of elements of A, one can pick U_n ∈ U_n so that {U_n : n ∈ ℕ} ∈ B;
• Sfin(A, B): for every sequence (U_n)_{n∈ℕ} of elements of A, one can pick finite F_n ⊆ U_n so that ⋃_n F_n ∈ B.

A property P of separable metrizable spaces is hereditary if every subset of a space with property P also has property P.

Question: Is any of the properties
S1(B_T, B_Γ), S1(B_T, B_T), S1(B_T, B_Ω), S1(B_T, B), Sfin(B_T, B_T), Sfin(B_T, B_Ω)
hereditary?

